WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:05.673
<v Examiner>What is a medication-drug interaction, and how can a nurse check for one?

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:06.607
<v Examiner>Give an example.

3
00:00:06.607 --> 00:00:07.191
<v Examiner>Two marks.

4
00:00:07.641 --> 00:00:12.100
The hook: before you assume it's fine, check the Formulary's Interactions tab.

5
00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:18.583
A medication-drug interaction is when a licit or illicit substance changes the effect of

6
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:21.668
a medication, or the two together cause harm.

7
00:00:21.668 --> 00:00:26.948
Her slide lists the substances you can look up: alcohol, marijuana, nicotine,

8
00:00:26.948 --> 00:00:30.513
sedatives like lorazepam, and opioids like morphine.

9
00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:35.211
How to check: the New Zealand Formulary's Interactions checker.

10
00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:40.876
Her activity was to look each of those substances up against the ten most prescribed

11
00:00:40.876 --> 00:00:42.562
medicines in New Zealand.

12
00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:48.361
Paracetamol, colecalciferol, atorvastatin, aspirin, omeprazole, metoprolol, ibuprofen,

13
00:00:48.361 --> 00:00:51.059
salbutamol, amoxicillin and candesartan.

14
00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:52.860
The example to write.

15
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.299
Alcohol taken with another depressant, an opioid, or benzodiazepines,

16
00:00:57.299 --> 00:00:59.101
raises the risk of overdose.

17
00:00:59.101 --> 00:01:04.441
One of the articles she points you to says that over a third of New Zealand's fatal

18
00:01:04.441 --> 00:01:06.179
overdoses involved alcohol,

19
00:01:06.179 --> 00:01:11.583
and the risk from opioids goes up when another depressant is taken at the same time.

20
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:17.623
Drug-drug interactions between recreational drugs are a worry too,

21
00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:20.544
and a chance for harm reduction education.

22
00:01:20.544 --> 00:01:24.925
Her slide points to the Know Your Stuff drug combination chart.

23
00:01:24.925 --> 00:01:30.141
Pusheen is on it, so you don't need the chart, but these were her examples.

24
00:01:30.141 --> 00:01:32.575
Alcohol and nitrous oxide: caution.

25
00:01:32.575 --> 00:01:35.009
Caffeine and amphetamines: caution.

26
00:01:35.009 --> 00:01:37.235
Alcohol and ketamine: dangerous.

27
00:01:37.235 --> 00:01:39.391
Opioids and cocaine: dangerous.

28
00:01:39.391 --> 00:01:43.355
And an antidepressant, an SSRI, with tramadol: dangerous.

29
00:01:43.355 --> 00:01:45.789
Young people often don't know that.

30
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:46.840
<v Examiner>Once more.

31
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.585
<v Examiner>Medication-drug interactions.

32
00:01:51.035 --> 00:01:56.009
A medication-drug interaction: a licit or illicit substance, like alcohol,

33
00:01:56.009 --> 00:02:01.387
marijuana or nicotine, changes a medicine's effect, or together they cause harm.

34
00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:05.084
Check the New Zealand Formulary's Interactions checker.

35
00:02:05.084 --> 00:02:09.655
Alcohol with another depressant, like an opioid, or benzodiazepines,

36
00:02:09.655 --> 00:02:11.537
raises the risk of overdose.
